<HEAD1>Step 1: Creating the source files</HEAD1>
Typically the source files are of three flavours: java files, manifestfile, GUI layout file and various assets (images, fonts
 etc). In our first example we shall have only one java file and one manifest file. The manifest file is the main entry point,
 and so must have an agreed upon name: AndroidManifest.xml.
<PRE>
<IFILE skip="0" labs="3,6,9">AndroidManifest.xml</IFILE>
</PRE>
At this point focus only on the three numbered lines. They work together to specify the initial
 entry point of the app. It is the class <CODE>my.first.MainActivity</CODE>. Note the dot before
 <CODE>MainActivity</CODE>  in the line labelled 3. The idea is that the attributes in lines 1 and 3 should give the full
 path of the class after concatenation. 
The line labelled 2 gives the label you'll see on your phone. 


Here is the java code:
<PRE>
<IFILE skip="0" labs="1,8,10">my/first/MainActivity.java</IFILE>
</PRE>
The basic structure should be clear. What we call an "app" from the user's perspective is an "Activity" from the java perspective.
 So our app extends Activity. We need an entry point (like main in C or desktop java). Here it is <CODE>onStartActivity</CODE>.
 It is passed something (some sort of environment in which to run the app). A <CODE>TextView</CODE>  is apparently a GUI
 component to show text. We place the component suitably and specify the fontsize, and then add the text to it. 

Obviously all these classes are declared inside some packages. The initial imports account for that. 

<HEAD1>Step 2: Compiling</HEAD1>
Next we need to compile the source files into some binary file that can be understood by our mobile phone.

We shall use the plain old <CODE>javac</CODE>  that we use to compile desktop java programs. But ofc ourse, it does not know
 about the classes like <CODE>Activity</CODE>  and <CODE>TextView</CODE>. The remedy is to pass it a jar file containing
 the codes for those classes. 
<PRE>javac -classpath $ANDROID_HOME/platforms/android-35/android.jar -d classes my/first/MainActivity.java</PRE>
Next we convert the class file to a dex file.
<PRE>
"$ANDROID_HOME/build-tools/34.0.0/d8" \
  --lib "$ANDROID_HOME/platforms/android-35/android.jar" \
  --min-api 23 \
my/first/*.class
</PRE>
This would produce <CODE>classes.dex</CODE>.
This takes care of the java file. Next we need to compile the manifest file:
<PRE>
"$ANDROID_HOME/build-tools/34.0.0/aapt2" link \
  -o base.apk \
  --manifest AndroidManifest.xml \
  -I "$ANDROID_HOME/platforms/android-35/android.jar" \
  --min-sdk-version 23 \
  --target-sdk-version 35
</PRE>
Basically, this command compiles the manifest file to base.apk. The exact technical term is
 "linking", but we may explore that difference later. 

Notice that so far the manifest and java file are compiled separately. We need to put them
 together. That is simple, since an apk file is just a zip file. We want to add <CODE>classes.dex</CODE>  to it.
<PRE>
zip -j base.apk dex/classes.dex
</PRE>
 
 Next we need to align it.

<PRE>

"$ANDROID_HOME/build-tools/34.0.0/zipalign" \
  -f -v 4 \
base.apk \
  aligned.apk
</PRE>

<HEAD1>Step 3: Signing</HEAD1>
First we need to create  signature. This is to be done only once. 
<PRE>
if test ! -e debug.keystore
  
keytool -genkeypair \
  -keystore debug.keystore \
  -alias androiddebugkey \
  -keyalg RSA \
  -keysize 2048 \
  -validity 10000 \
  -storepass android \
  -keypass android \
  -dname "CN=Android Debug,O=Android,C=US"
</PRE>
And finally put the signature of the apk file:
<PRE>
"$ANDROID_HOME/build-tools/34.0.0/apksigner" sign \
  --ks debug.keystore \
  --ks-key-alias androiddebugkey \
  --ks-pass pass:android \
  --key-pass pass:android \
  --out app-debug.apk \
  aligned.apk
</PRE>
